From a31aa3f66379bc4385da05ff6034f049fc888570 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Pratap Sidhu <pratapkevin8@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 13:22:47 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] [NUI] Skeleton injection at the title runtime's frame
 callback

--nui_skeleton_inject=test replaces every skeleton the title's own
tracker fits with one xenia supplies, so that a skeleton from another
tracker can drive the title ("Option B"). The only source so far is a
synthetic swaying, jumping test figure.

The site is found by code, not by address: NuiSkeletonGetNextFrame by
PS5X360's signature, then the runtime state block, slot ring and frame
offset read out of its instructions, then the one thunk that calls
through a function pointer in that block, which the frame producer
calls once a frame is filled and before it is published. Each step must
match exactly once. The thunk is bound like an import (sc 2; blr plus an
extern handler) at message 0x2B003, before the camera worker runs. The
handler rewrites the six skeleton slots, keeping the frame header and
each slot's enrollment and user index words, and still calls the
runtime's callback when one is set. NuiSkeletonGetNextFrame, the HUD
message 0x2B002, the Kinect Guide and the play-frame log all see the
injected skeleton.

Also corrects the X_NUI_SKELETON_DATA comment: the runtime's identity
pass writes the enrollment and user index (-1 and 0xFE seen).

Tested: xenia-nui-tests pass (86 cases, 37259 assertions; 5 new).
Kinect Adventures (4D5308ED, module hash FC152DDFE29E856E) on a
webcam-synthesised replay: site found and bound; every play-frame line
carries the test figure with a stable id; the title completed its
play-space setup ("GOOD SPACE!") within 90 s, where the same replay
without injection was still at "MOVE BACK AS FAR AS YOU CAN" with the
tracker's ids changing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
